{"@context":["https://litlfred.github.io/folio-assistant/ns/content/v1.jsonld",{"@base":"https://litlfred.github.io/folio/"}],"@id":"papers/quantum-observable-universe/blocks/prop-q-casimir-unique","@type":["folio-assistant-core:Proposition","doco:Section"],"label":"prop:q-casimir-unique","kind":"proposition","title":"Uniqueness of the $q$-Casimir element up to scalar","uses":["papers/quantum-observable-universe/blocks/prop-q-casimir-exists"],"proofs":["papers/quantum-observable-universe/blocks/prf-q-casimir-unique"],"tags":["uniqueness","casimir","central-element"],"leanRef":"qou:QOU.QCasimir.unique_up_to_scalar","text":"q-casimir-unique.md","leanSource":"folio/quantum-observable-universe/braids-and-knots/q-casimir-unique.lean","provenance":"authored","html":"<a id=\"prop:q-casimir-unique\"></a><p>The <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">q</code>-Casimir element <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">C_q</code> is the unique central element of <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\mathcal{U}_q(\\mathfrak{su}(2))</code> of the form <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">FE + \\phi(K, K^{-1})</code> whose eigenvalue on the spin-<code class=\"language-math math-inline\">j</code> irrep is <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">[j]_q [j+1]_q</code>. Any two such central elements differ by a scalar multiple of the identity.</p>\n<p>This uniqueness result regarding the Joseph--Letzter centre is standard.</p>\n","source":"folio/quantum-observable-universe/braids-and-knots/q-casimir-unique.md","heading":"Proposition","number":"6.9.6"}
